Hi Bruno,

First of all let me welcome you to Kernel Janitors. I'm just a simple=20
linux user like you trying do dig up into kernel development willing to=20
be usefull to the community somehow. As such user, I'd like to give you=20
some tips that i've found myself usefull while searching how to start also.

- The first place you should like to take a look is at kernel janitor's=20
home page at www.kerneljanitors.org.
It as a bunch of guide lines, best pratices and documentation that will=20
help you start with the right foot, avoinding those nasty avoidable=20
newbie errors.
All this information as been gathered by the kernel janitors=20
contributors and certainly is a very good place to start.

- A very good reading in linux device drivers development is the Linux=20
Device Drivers 3rd Edition (O'Reilly). It's a very well writen book,=20
clear and objective full of pratical examples. It as the advantage to=20
have a very close vision of the kernel develepment community itself=20
since it has the co-authory of renown kernel developers, such as Greg=20
Kroah-Hartman. Thankfully the authors and O'Reilly have made the book=20
freely available and you can get it here http://lwn.net/Kernel/LDD3/

- I've personally liked very much of Understanding The Linux Kernel=20
(O'Reilly) book. It does not focus on device driver, but it gives a very=20
technical vision of how the linux kernel is put together. It's a bit=20
more technical tough, and I used primarily for reference. Sadly this=20
isn't a freely available book.

- Kernel newbies wiki (http://www.kernelnewbies.org/) is a very good=20
resource also!

- You can find the help of other KJ contributers at #kerneljanitor=20
(irc.kernelnewbies.org), on free tuits I guess everyone will be glad to=20
help you.

- linux/Documention is a crucial place for reference also! Many of the=20
things you'll find in other documentation, you'll almost shure find it=20
there with a more technical orientation.

- Other resources I like to search are the slide presentations made by=20
the kernel developers since it gives you a very good insight how the=20
kernel main developers aknowledge certain processes of the kernel=20
development. It's a good way to understand how the whole thing works and=20
it's philosophy/politics.

Greg's site is a good resource (http://www.kroah.com). On the kernel=20
janitors site you can find also a few Randy's presentations. Maybe=20
someone would like to share other references too. I'd be thankful  also!

Hope you find usefull and happy hacking,

Artur D'Assump=E7=E3o (flea)



